//@ known-failure: writes through an exclusive reference without declaring `with Mut`, so E035 is
//@ known-failure: correct and this must keep failing. What changed is that the message now says
//@ known-failure: WHICH call demanded the effect:
//@ known-failure:
//@ known-failure:   before: error[E035] at 0..135: effect not declared ... (missing: Mut)
//@ known-failure:   after:  error[E035] at 0..135: effect not declared ... (missing: Mut)
//@ known-failure:                                  -- required by `needs_mut`
//@ known-failure:
//@ known-failure: The span cannot substitute: all 230 E035 on a self-compile of compiler/main.sio
//@ known-failure: carry span.start == 0, and no diagnostic in this compiler prints span.file_id,
//@ known-failure: so an offset alone cannot say which of 112 closure files it belongs to.
//@ known-failure:
//@ known-failure: The name is the CALLEE, not the enclosing function, deliberately. The enclosing
//@ known-failure: function is what you edit, so it would read better — but the checker does not
//@ known-failure: track it, and a `current_fn_name` field would have to stay in sync across 13
//@ known-failure: assignment sites and 6 save/restore pairs. One missed restore prints a STALE
//@ known-failure: name, and a confidently wrong attribution is worse than none. The callee name
//@ known-failure: travels out of the same FnSig as the effect list, so it cannot desynchronise.

struct Box2 {
    v: i64,
}

fn needs_mut(b: &!Box2) with Mut {
    (*b).v = 1
}

fn caller_missing_mut(b: &!Box2) {
    needs_mut(b)
}

fn main() with IO, Mut, Panic, Div {
    var x = Box2 { v: 0 }
    caller_missing_mut(&!x)
    print_int(x.v)
    println(" done")
}
